# Summary
Introduced. The pre-existing local debug endpoint was already unauthenticated, but this commit newly routes the bucket-delete debug path to bucket_delete_debug_snapshot and formats bucket row and pending command details into the HTTP response. That expands the unauthenticated information disclosure from delete-attempt status to private bucket metadata and possibly unrelated bucket names in the same metadata PG.
When ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT is enabled, requests under /__argmin/debug/ are handled before the normal S3 authentication and request-admission path. This commit changes the bucket-delete debug endpoint from returning only the durable delete-attempt outcome to returning a broader BucketDeleteDebugSnapshot. The snapshot now performs a raw bucket lookup and exposes whether a bucket row exists, its lifecycle state and generation counters, durable write-drain details, and the current pending metadata command's target_bucket. Because the endpoint accepts an arbitrary bucket name from the URL and does not authenticate the requester, any local process able to reach the loopback listener can confirm guessed private bucket names and, while a pending command exists for the bucket PG, may learn another bucket name from target_bucket even if it did not request that bucket. The endpoint is disabled by default and configuration restricts it to loopback listeners, so this does not expose object data or credentials to the network by default, but it is still a confidentiality regression for deployments enabling local debug diagnostics.

# Resolution
Fixed for production/release builds by the local-debug endpoint hardening commits:

- `45497afe` gates local debug route dispatch and helper code behind `cfg(any(test, feature = "local-debug-endpoints"))`, and rejects `ARGMIN_LOCAL_DEBUG_ENDPOINT=1` at startup unless the binary was compiled with that non-production support.
- `73a36bcd` makes `local-debug-endpoints` a debug/test-only feature by adding a release-build `compile_error!`, and updates UAT smoke handling so debug-hook smokes cannot be run against release or externally supplied binaries.

The bucket-delete debug snapshot remains available in explicit debug/test builds for diagnostics, but release artifacts cannot include or enable the unauthenticated debug route. Production default builds reject the environment variable before startup.

